<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Teniente</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Teniente is the largest underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine in the world, in the Andes near Rancagua in central Chile. It was developed from 1905 by the Braden Copper Company, later owned by Kennecott, and supported the mining town of Sewell. Nationalised with the other large copper mines in 1971, it is now operated by the state company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codelco">Codelco</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-teniente.jpg" alt="El Teniente" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Schubert · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fundici%C3%B3n_de_cobre_en_El_Teniente.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
